// QA Learning Hub — Quiz question banks
// Each question: { q, options: [4 strings], correct: index, explain }
window.QAHUB_QUIZZES = {
  "manual-testing": [
    { q: "According to the IBM System Sciences Institute, fixing a bug found at implementation vs. at the design phase costs roughly how much more?", options: ["2x", "6x", "15x", "It costs the same"], correct: 1, explain: "The IBM System Sciences Institute found that a defect caught at implementation costs about 6x more to fix than one caught at design — the core argument for testing early." },
    { q: "What share of projects cite UX validation as a primary reason for manual testing?", options: ["Over 70%", "About 10%", "Exactly 50%", "Under 5%"], correct: 0, explain: "Over 70% of projects cite UX — placement, aesthetics, and feedback — as a primary reason human testers are needed." },
    { q: "Which classification of manual testing is based on having NO knowledge of the application's internal code?", options: ["White Box Testing", "Grey Box Testing", "Black Box Testing", "Unit Testing"], correct: 2, explain: "Black Box Testing verifies functionality using only requirements and specifications, without knowledge of internal code." },
    { q: "Which of these is one of the five Primary Goals of manual testing listed in this tutorial?", options: ["Automation Coverage", "Regression Confidence", "Load Balancing", "Code Refactoring"], correct: 1, explain: "The five primary goals are Bug Identification, Requirement Validation, User Experience Assurance, Regression Confidence, and Quality Delivery." },
    { q: "Which of these is listed as a disadvantage of manual testing for large, complex applications?", options: ["Too fast to catch bugs", "Not Scalable", "Requires heavy coding skill", "Cannot involve human judgment"], correct: 1, explain: "Manual testing is time-consuming and doesn't scale well for large or complex applications — that's why automation exists alongside it." },
    { q: "In the Hospital Management System case study, what is the manual tester primarily validating?", options: ["Server response times under load", "That the app compiles without errors", "Real-world workflow sequences across registration, scheduling and billing", "Source code style conventions"], correct: 2, explain: "The case study is about validating that data entered in one workflow (e.g. registration) correctly reaches downstream screens (billing, doctor views) — a real-world usage sequence." },
    { q: "Which testing approach, per the Manual vs. Automation comparison, requires programming skill in addition to testing knowledge?", options: ["Manual Testing", "Automation Testing", "Neither", "Both equally, with no distinction"], correct: 1, explain: "Automation Testing requires programming plus testing knowledge, while manual testing needs domain and testing knowledge but no coding." },
    { q: "What is the key difference between defect Severity and Priority?", options: ["They mean exactly the same thing", "Severity is the technical impact; Priority is how urgently it needs fixing from a business perspective", "Severity is set by product, Priority is set by the tester", "Priority only applies to automated tests"], correct: 1, explain: "Severity and Priority are independent axes — a high-severity bug in a rarely-used area can be low priority, while a low-severity but highly visible bug can be high priority." },
    { q: "What is the most common reason a bug report gets bounced back to the reporter instead of fixed?", options: ["The report is too short", "Missing steps to reproduce", "The severity was set too high", "It was found by a junior tester"], correct: 1, explain: "Without clear, exact repro steps, a developer often can't confirm or diagnose the issue, so the report gets sent back for more detail before it can be fixed." }
  ],
  "test-techniques": [
    { q: "For a valid input range of 1–10, which values would Boundary Value Analysis test?", options: ["1, 5, 10", "0, 1, 2, 9, 10, 11", "-10, 0, 10, 20", "Every integer from 1 to 10"], correct: 1, explain: "BVA tests just inside, at, and just outside each boundary: 0, 1, 2 (lower boundary) and 9, 10, 11 (upper boundary)." },
    { q: "Which technique divides inputs into valid and invalid groups and tests one representative value from each?", options: ["Boundary Value Analysis", "Decision Table Testing", "Equivalence Class Partitioning", "State Transition Testing"], correct: 2, explain: "Equivalence Class Partitioning (ECP) assumes all values in a class behave alike, so testing one representative per class is enough." },
    { q: "Which testing principle states that running the same tests repeatedly eventually stops finding new bugs?", options: ["Defect Clustering", "Pesticide Paradox", "Absence-of-Errors Fallacy", "Exhaustive Testing is Impossible"], correct: 1, explain: "The Pesticide Paradox: reusing the same tests makes them 'immune' to finding new defects, so tests must be reviewed and updated." },
    { q: "A small number of modules containing most of a system's defects is described by which principle?", options: ["Defect Clustering", "Early Testing Saves Time and Cost", "Testing is Context-Dependent", "Pesticide Paradox"], correct: 0, explain: "Defect Clustering observes that defects tend to concentrate in a small number of modules." },
    { q: "Which technique would you use to test a 'Submit' button that only enables when all form fields are filled?", options: ["Error Guessing", "Boundary Value Analysis", "Decision Table Testing", "Path Testing"], correct: 2, explain: "Decision Table Testing maps combinations of input conditions (which fields are filled) to expected outputs (button enabled or not)." },
    { q: "In the login/PIN State Transition example, what happens after 3 incorrect PIN attempts in a row?", options: ["Access Granted", "The counter resets to 0", "Account Blocked", "Nothing changes"], correct: 2, explain: "Per the state table, three consecutive incorrect attempts move the system to the 'Account Blocked' state." },
    { q: "Which technique relies purely on tester intuition and historical defect data, with no formal steps?", options: ["Error Guessing", "Equivalence Class Partitioning", "Decision Table Testing", "Boundary Value Analysis"], correct: 0, explain: "Error Guessing has no formal steps — it depends entirely on the tester's experience and knowledge of where bugs tend to occur." },
    { q: "What is the best-practice recommendation for choosing a test design technique?", options: ["Always use Boundary Value Analysis only", "Rely on a single technique for consistency", "Combine multiple techniques rather than relying on one", "Skip technique selection and test everything exhaustively"], correct: 2, explain: "No single technique guarantees full defect detection, so combining multiple techniques is recommended." },
    { q: "Why is pairwise testing useful for a feature with many independent input parameters?", options: ["It tests every possible combination, guaranteeing full coverage", "It covers every pair of parameter values with far fewer test cases, since most defects come from two-parameter interactions", "It only works for boundary values", "It replaces the need for equivalence partitioning entirely"], correct: 1, explain: "Pairwise testing is built on the observation that most interaction defects involve just two parameters, so covering every pair (not every full combination) catches most issues far more cheaply." },
    { q: "What does Use Case Testing derive test cases from?", options: ["Random data generation", "Documented step-by-step interactions between an actor and the system, including alternate/exception flows", "Source code branch coverage", "Boundary values of a single input field"], correct: 1, explain: "Use Case Testing builds test cases directly from the main success scenario and alternate/exception flows of a documented use case, validating an end-to-end workflow from the user's perspective." }
  ],
  "testing-types": [
    { q: "What defines a 'type' of software testing, per this module?", options: ["Only the tool used to run it", "A classification with its own objective, strategy, and deliverables", "The programming language of the system under test", "How long the test takes to execute"], correct: 1, explain: "A testing type is a classification — like Accessibility Testing — with a specific objective, strategy and deliverables." },
    { q: "Which category groups testing types like Unit Testing, Branch Testing, and Path Testing?", options: ["Functional", "Non-Functional", "Structural (White Box)", "Change-Related"], correct: 2, explain: "These are all White Box / Structural techniques that examine the internal code paths of a system." },
    { q: "Regression Testing and Smoke Testing best belong to which category in the module's taxonomy?", options: ["Specialized", "Change-Related", "Structural", "Non-Functional"], correct: 1, explain: "Both are triggered by a change (a fix, a new build) and check that the change didn't break anything — Change-Related." },
    { q: "Which of these is part of the curated 'Top 20 Testing Types Every QA Should Know'?", options: ["Fault Injection Testing", "Usability Testing", "Orthogonal Array Testing", "Modularity-driven Testing"], correct: 1, explain: "Usability Testing is one of the 20 high-value, recognizable entries the module highlights as a starting point." },
    { q: "Accessibility Testing is best classified under which category?", options: ["Specialized", "Structural", "Change-Related", "Functional"], correct: 0, explain: "Accessibility Testing addresses a targeted, specialized concern — usability for people with disabilities." },
    { q: "Where does the full 105+ term testing types catalogue live in this app?", options: ["Only in this module's text", "The Glossary", "The Final Exam", "It isn't included"], correct: 1, explain: "The module links out to the Glossary, which holds the full extracted catalogue of testing types." },
    { q: "Why does non-functional testing need an explicit threshold defined before testing starts?", options: ["It doesn't — 'good enough' is always obvious", "Without one, 'how well' has no pass/fail line, just opinions", "Thresholds are only needed for functional testing", "Thresholds slow down the testing process unnecessarily"], correct: 1, explain: "A statement like 'page loads in under 2s at 500 concurrent users' gives a clear pass/fail line — without an agreed threshold, non-functional results are just subjective opinions." },
    { q: "Why is pseudo-localization (artificially lengthened/accented strings) useful before real translations exist?", options: ["It replaces the need for real translation entirely", "It catches layout breaks caused by text expansion early", "It tests server performance under load", "It only checks currency formatting"], correct: 1, explain: "Pseudo-localization surfaces UI layout problems caused by longer translated text before real translations are even written, catching a whole class of bugs early." }
  ],
  "adhoc-testing": [
    { q: "Which of these is NOT a recognized type of Ad Hoc Testing in this tutorial?", options: ["Buddy Testing", "Pair Testing", "Monkey Testing", "Regression Testing"], correct: 3, explain: "Regression Testing is a change-related testing type, not a form of Ad Hoc Testing. Buddy, Pair, and Monkey Testing are the three types covered." },
    { q: "Ad Hoc Testing is best described as which kind of approach?", options: ["A white-box approach requiring source code access", "A black-box approach with no prepared test cases", "An automated regression suite", "A formal, documented certification process"], correct: 1, explain: "Ad Hoc Testing is informal, unscripted, and black-box — no test cases are prepared in advance." },
    { q: "Which is a listed 'When to Perform It' scenario for Ad Hoc Testing?", options: ["Only during initial requirement gathering", "When short on time", "Only after the product has shipped", "Never during beta testing"], correct: 1, explain: "Being short on time is one of several good moments for ad hoc testing, alongside early development, before/after formal testing, deep feature exploration, usability checks, and beta testing." },
    { q: "What is a key disadvantage of Ad Hoc Testing?", options: ["It requires extensive documentation", "It's hard to reproduce or repeat", "It cannot find any edge-case defects", "It is always slower than scripted testing"], correct: 1, explain: "Because there's no script or record, ad hoc test sessions are hard to reproduce exactly — a real traceability weakness." },
    { q: "Which best practice is recommended for Ad Hoc Testing?", options: ["Avoid recording defects to save time", "Test random modules with no risk assessment", "Target key/high-risk modules and record every defect found", "Never pair with another tester"], correct: 2, explain: "Best practices include having good business knowledge, targeting high-risk modules, recording every defect, and pairing for a fresh perspective." },
    { q: "In Monkey Testing, what characterizes the input given to the application?", options: ["Carefully scripted boundary values", "Random, unstructured inputs", "Only valid equivalence-class inputs", "Inputs generated from a decision table"], correct: 1, explain: "Monkey Testing throws random, unstructured inputs at the application to see if it breaks." },
    { q: "What is a 'bug bash', as used to integrate ad hoc testing into Agile sprints?", options: ["A formal, scripted regression suite", "A time-boxed session where the whole team ad hoc tests a build together", "A meeting to prioritize the backlog", "A tool for automatically generating bug reports"], correct: 1, explain: "A bug bash is a time-boxed (often 30-60 minute) session where the whole team, not just QA, tests a build together — spreading product knowledge and catching bugs individual habits might miss." },
    { q: "Why should even informal ad hoc testing sessions be time-boxed?", options: ["Time-boxing is not actually useful for ad hoc testing", "It keeps the session focused rather than wandering, even without a formal script", "It guarantees full test coverage", "It replaces the need for logging defects"], correct: 1, explain: "A stated time limit (e.g. 20 minutes on the new checkout flow) keeps an unscripted session focused even though there's no formal test case to follow." }
  ],
  "exploratory-testing": [
    { q: "In SBTM, what comes immediately after defining the Test Charter?", options: ["Debrief", "Review Results", "Time Box the session", "Create a Bug Taxonomy"], correct: 2, explain: "The SBTM order is: Bug Taxonomy → Test Charter → Time Box the session → Review Results → Debrief." },
    { q: "Per the Scripted vs. Exploratory comparison table, exploratory testing is best described as...", options: ["Confirmatory", "Investigative", "About controlling tests", "Directed only from requirements"], correct: 1, explain: "The table contrasts Scripted Testing (confirmatory) with Exploratory Testing (investigative)." },
    { q: "In exploratory testing, test design and test execution happen...", options: ["In strictly separate phases, design first", "Simultaneously", "Only after the product ships", "Only by developers, never testers"], correct: 1, explain: "Exploratory testing is defined by simultaneous test design and execution — a 'thinking' activity of investigation and learning." },
    { q: "Which is listed as a con of Exploratory Testing?", options: ["It requires heavy upfront documentation", "It's hard to measure coverage", "It cannot adapt to changing requirements", "It never finds new bugs"], correct: 1, explain: "Because sessions aren't pre-scripted, measuring exact coverage and reproducing sessions exactly is difficult." },
    { q: "When is Exploratory Testing especially recommended, per this module?", options: ["Only for fully documented, stable requirements", "When requirements are missing or ambiguous", "Only in strict regulatory/compliance audits", "Only for testers with no domain experience"], correct: 1, explain: "Exploratory testing shines when requirements are missing/ambiguous, in early iterations, on critical apps needing deep investigation, and with experienced testers." },
    { q: "Which phrase from the comparison table describes Scripted Testing?", options: ["Like having a spontaneous conversation", "The tester's mind is in control", "Like reading a speech from a draft", "About improving test design"], correct: 2, explain: "Scripted Testing is 'like reading a speech from a draft' — the script is in control, versus exploratory testing's spontaneous conversation." },
    { q: "In the SFDPOT heuristic, what does the 'O' stand for?", options: ["Objectives", "Output", "Operations — how the product is actually used in the real world", "Optimization"], correct: 2, explain: "SFDPOT's O is Operations — how the product is actually used in the real world, including atypical usage, not just its documented function." },
    { q: "In the HICCUPPS oracle heuristic, what question does 'User expectations' help a tester answer?", options: ["Whether the code compiles without errors", "Whether an observed behavior is a bug, by checking if it matches what a reasonable user would expect", "How fast the system responds under load", "Which browser the test was run on"], correct: 1, explain: "HICCUPPS is a set of oracle heuristics for deciding whether unexpected behavior counts as a bug when there's no explicit spec — 'User expectations' asks whether it matches what a reasonable user would expect." }
  ],
  "rest-api-testing": [
    { q: "Which HTTP method both creates a new entity AND can update an existing one?", options: ["GET", "POST", "PUT", "DELETE"], correct: 2, explain: "PUT can create a new entity or update an existing one, depending on whether the resource already exists." },
    { q: "Which HTTP method should never have side effects on server data?", options: ["GET", "POST", "PUT", "DELETE"], correct: 0, explain: "GET is meant purely for retrieving data via a URI and should have no side effects on server-side data." },
    { q: "What does REST stand for?", options: ["Reliable State Transfer", "Representational State Transfer", "Remote Endpoint Service Testing", "Redundant State Transmission"], correct: 1, explain: "REST stands for Representational State Transfer, an architectural style for web services." },
    { q: "Which HTTP method is typically used for form submissions and file uploads that create new data?", options: ["GET", "POST", "DELETE", "HEAD"], correct: 1, explain: "POST creates a new entity or sends data to the server, such as form submissions or file uploads." },
    { q: "Which tools are named in this module as common ways to test a REST API?", options: ["Selenium, JMeter, LoadRunner", "Advanced REST Client, Postman, cURL", "Excel, Word, PowerPoint", "Git, Docker, Kubernetes"], correct: 1, explain: "The module names Advanced REST Client, Postman, and cURL as common REST API testing tools." },
    { q: "What should you validate after submitting an API request, according to the step-by-step workflow?", options: ["Only the HTTP status code", "Only whether the server didn't crash", "Status code, response body/schema, and headers", "Nothing — a successful send is enough"], correct: 2, explain: "The workflow calls for validating the status code, the response body or schema, and the headers against expected results." },
    { q: "Which of these is listed as a genuine challenge of API testing?", options: ["Too much UI to click through", "Verifying just the output without a UI", "APIs never need sequencing of calls", "There are too few HTTP methods to choose from"], correct: 1, explain: "Without a UI, verifying just the output response requires different validation skills than typical UI testing." },
    { q: "When testing a JWT-based authentication flow, what should be verified about signature validation?", options: ["Signatures don't need to be checked if the token looks well-formed", "A tampered token must be rejected — the server must not trust claims without verifying the signature", "Only the token's expiry matters, not its signature", "JWTs cannot be tested for security issues"], correct: 1, explain: "A JWT's claims are only trustworthy if the signature is verified — a server that trusts claims without checking the signature can be tricked by a tampered token." },
    { q: "What is a testing concern unique to GraphQL that doesn't apply the same way to typical REST endpoints?", options: ["GraphQL cannot return JSON", "Queries requesting excessive or deeply nested data can pose a denial-of-service risk", "GraphQL has no concept of authentication", "GraphQL only supports GET requests"], correct: 1, explain: "Because a GraphQL client can request deeply nested or excessive data in a single query, testing needs to check the schema guards against queries that could overload the server." }
  ],
  "istqb-ctfl": [
    { q: "Which of the seven testing principles states that running the same tests repeatedly eventually stops finding new bugs?", options: ["Defect Clustering", "Tests Wear Out", "Pesticide Paradox is a myth", "Exhaustive Testing is Impossible"], correct: 1, explain: "\"Tests wear out\" — reusing the same tests repeatedly makes them progressively less effective at finding new defects." },
    { q: "A human mistake that leads to a flaw in a work product is called a defect. What is the mistake itself called?", options: ["Fault", "Failure", "Error", "Root Cause"], correct: 2, explain: "An error (human mistake) introduces a defect (flaw); when executed, a defect can cause a failure (observable wrong behavior). The underlying reason is the root cause." },
    { q: "Which review type is the most formal, using defined roles, entry/exit criteria, and checklists?", options: ["Informal Review", "Walkthrough", "Technical Review", "Inspection"], correct: 3, explain: "Inspection is the most formal of the four review types, followed in decreasing formality by Technical Review, Walkthrough, and Informal Review." },
    { q: "Which white-box technique checks whether every executable statement has been run at least once?", options: ["Branch Testing", "Statement Testing", "Decision Table Testing", "State Transition Testing"], correct: 1, explain: "Statement Testing/Coverage asks whether every executable statement in the code has been executed at least once during testing." },
    { q: "In risk-based testing, risk level is typically calculated from which two factors?", options: ["Cost and duration", "Likelihood and impact", "Severity and priority", "Coverage and complexity"], correct: 1, explain: "Risk = likelihood x impact. Higher-risk areas — more likely to fail and more damaging if they do — get prioritized." },
    { q: "Which model favors many fast, low-level automated tests and fewer slow, high-level UI tests?", options: ["The Testing Quadrants", "The V-Model", "The Test Pyramid", "The Defect Lifecycle"], correct: 2, explain: "The Test Pyramid recommends a broad base of fast, low-level automated tests (unit) and progressively fewer slower, higher-level tests (integration, UI/E2E)." },
    { q: "Which of these is a commonly cited risk (not benefit) of test automation?", options: ["Repeatability", "Objective results", "Maintenance cost / false sense of security", "Speed"], correct: 2, explain: "Repeatability, objective results, and speed are benefits of automation; maintenance cost and a false sense of security are commonly cited risks." },
    { q: "Which category of test technique relies on tester intuition and historical defect data rather than a formal, documented procedure?", options: ["Black-box", "White-box", "Experience-based", "Structure-based"], correct: 2, explain: "Experience-based techniques — Error Guessing, Exploratory Testing, Checklist-Based Testing — draw on tester intuition and history rather than formal derivation from a test basis." },
    { q: "What distinguishes a test level from a test type, per the syllabus?", options: ["They are two names for the same thing", "Test level describes where testing happens (e.g. unit, system); test type describes what is tested (e.g. functional, non-functional)", "Test type only applies to Agile projects", "Test level only applies to manual testing"], correct: 1, explain: "Test levels (component, integration, system, acceptance) and test types (functional, non-functional, structural, change-related) are independent dimensions — a test type can be applied at any test level." },
    { q: "What is the purpose of exit criteria in test planning?", options: ["To decide who attends the kickoff meeting", "To define the conditions that must be met for a test activity to be considered complete", "To assign severity to defects", "To select which HTTP method to test"], correct: 1, explain: "Exit criteria define when a test activity is done; entry criteria define the conditions needed before it can start." },
    { q: "According to the study-tips lesson, what's the most effective use of limited exam prep time?", options: ["Spend equal time on every K-level equally", "Focus on K2/K3 material, since that's where most exam questions live", "Only study K1 (recall) terms", "Memorize the book's exact exam-logistics tables"], correct: 1, explain: "K1 terms just need recognition, but K2/K3 material requires understanding and application — and that's where most exam questions are concentrated." },
    { q: "What kind of trap does a 'best answer' distractor question typically set?", options: ["It gives one obviously wrong answer and three identical correct ones", "It gives four technically-true statements but only one is the best fit for the specific scenario described", "It never has a correct answer at all", "It only appears in the Module 7 quiz, never on the real exam"], correct: 1, explain: "A common ISTQB-style trap presents several technically true statements, but only one is the best answer for the exact scenario given — reading the scenario carefully matters more than knowing the facts alone." }
  ],
  "qa-toolbox": [
    { q: "Which tool is described as running inside the browser, with a strong developer-focused debugging experience for JS/TS web testing?", options: ["Selenium WebDriver", "Cypress", "Appium", "REST Assured"], correct: 1, explain: "Cypress runs inside the browser itself, which gives it a distinctive, highly interactive debugging experience compared to WebDriver-based tools." },
    { q: "Which tool extends the WebDriver protocol to automate native and hybrid mobile apps?", options: ["Playwright", "Robot Framework", "Appium", "k6"], correct: 2, explain: "Appium extends the WebDriver protocol so the same automation approach used for web can drive native and hybrid mobile apps on iOS and Android." },
    { q: "Which performance testing tool scripts load tests in JavaScript and is CLI-first, built for easy CI/CD integration?", options: ["JMeter", "k6", "Gatling", "SoapUI"], correct: 1, explain: "k6 scripts are written in JavaScript and the tool is designed CLI-first, making it a natural fit for CI/CD pipelines." },
    { q: "A metric tracking defects found in production versus defects caught before release is called...", options: ["Defect Density", "Test Coverage", "Escape Rate", "Flakiness Rate"], correct: 2, explain: "Escape Rate (escaped defects) is the sharpest signal of whether a test strategy is actually catching problems before users do." },
    { q: "Why is a rising flakiness rate in an automated test suite a serious problem, beyond the failures themselves?", options: ["It has no real impact on the team", "It erodes trust in the suite, so real failures start getting ignored", "It always means the application has a security flaw", "It only affects manual testers"], correct: 1, explain: "Once a suite is known to be flaky, failures get dismissed by default — including real ones — which quietly destroys the value of having automation at all." },
    { q: "Which pair are two of the four DORA metrics mentioned in this module?", options: ["Defect Density and Escape Rate", "Lead Time for Changes and Deployment Frequency", "MTTD and Test Coverage", "Pass Rate and Flakiness Rate"], correct: 1, explain: "Lead Time for Changes and Deployment Frequency are two of the four DORA metrics; QA cycle time is often a major factor in lead time." },
    { q: "In an interview, what's the strongest way to answer \"describe a bug you found that others missed\"?", options: ["Give a vague, general answer to avoid sounding boastful", "Describe a specific real example: what led you there, repro steps, impact, and resolution", "Say you don't remember any specific bugs", "List as many bug types as possible without detail"], correct: 1, explain: "Interviewers are testing for specificity and real investigative process, not a dramatic story — a concrete example with repro steps and impact stands out." },
    { q: "What is the key difference between verification and validation?", options: ["They mean exactly the same thing", "Verification asks if the product meets the spec; validation asks if it meets the real user need", "Verification is manual, validation is always automated", "Validation only applies to performance testing"], correct: 1, explain: "Verification checks \"are we building the product right?\" against the spec; validation checks \"are we building the right product?\" against the actual user need." },
    { q: "Which language is described as a natural fit for automating a web app when that app's own frontend is written in it, letting testers share tooling with developers?", options: ["Java", "SQL", "JavaScript / TypeScript", "Bash"], correct: 2, explain: "JavaScript/TypeScript is a natural fit for testing JS/TS web apps — testers can read the app's own codebase and reuse the same tooling as the dev team." },
    { q: "What is the main risk of writing test automation in a language nobody else on the team knows?", options: ["It runs slower than other languages", "It becomes an orphaned, unmaintained suite within a year", "It cannot be integrated into CI/CD", "It is illegal under most open-source licenses"], correct: 1, explain: "Automation is only as valuable as the team's ability to maintain it — code nobody can read or update tends to rot and get abandoned." },
    { q: "What problem does self-healing test automation solve?", options: ["It writes entire test suites with no human input", "It automatically adjusts UI locators when the page markup changes, instead of breaking the test", "It fixes bugs in the application under test", "It replaces the need for a QA team"], correct: 1, explain: "Self-healing automation (e.g. Testim, Healenium) adjusts element locators when the DOM changes, so minor markup tweaks don't break every test that touches that element." },
    { q: "Why do exact-match assertions typically stop working when testing an LLM-powered feature?", options: ["LLMs never produce text output", "LLM output is non-deterministic, so the same input can produce different valid outputs", "Exact-match assertions are always too slow", "LLMs cannot be tested at all"], correct: 1, explain: "Because LLM output varies between runs even for the same input, testing shifts from exact-match assertions to rubric-based evaluation of whether the output is good enough, not identical to a fixed string." },
    { q: "What should a tester do before treating an AI-generated test as finished?", options: ["Nothing — if it runs and passes, it's done", "Confirm a human has verified it actually asserts the right thing, not just that it executes", "Delete it and write it manually instead, always", "Only check that it uses the newest AI model"], correct: 1, explain: "AI-generated tests are a useful first draft, but only a human can judge whether the test is actually checking the behavior that matters to the business." },
    { q: "What share of accessibility issues do automated scanners (like axe or Lighthouse) typically catch on their own?", options: ["100% — automated scanning is sufficient by itself", "Roughly 30-40% — manual testing (keyboard-only navigation, screen readers) is still required", "0% — automated tools can't check accessibility at all", "Exactly 50%, per the WCAG standard"], correct: 1, explain: "Automated scanners are excellent at objective checks like contrast ratios, but catch only about 30-40% of issues — manual keyboard and screen-reader testing is still needed for full coverage." },
    { q: "Under WCAG, what do the letters POUR stand for?", options: ["Priority, Objective, Usability, Reliability", "Perceivable, Operable, Understandable, Robust", "Performance, Operations, Usage, Reporting", "Public, Open, Universal, Reviewed"], correct: 1, explain: "WCAG organizes accessibility requirements around four principles: content must be Perceivable, Operable, Understandable, and Robust." }
  ],
  "test-architecture": [
    { q: "What is the key difference between the Page Object Model and the Screenplay Pattern?", options: ["They are exactly the same thing with different names", "POM encapsulates each page's locators/actions in a class; Screenplay models tests as an Actor with Abilities, Tasks, and Questions using composition", "Screenplay only works for API testing, not UI testing", "POM is newer and always preferred over Screenplay"], correct: 1, explain: "POM organizes automation around page classes; Screenplay uses an actor-centric, composition-based model (Abilities, Tasks, Questions) that tends to scale better for large, complex suites." },
    { q: "In a layered ('hybrid') test automation architecture, what is the purpose of separating the data layer from the logic layer?", options: ["It has no real benefit, just extra files", "So a locator or test-data change doesn't ripple into the readable test cases themselves", "So tests run faster on the same hardware", "So only developers can write tests"], correct: 1, explain: "Separating test cases, action/logic code, and data keeps a locator or data change from breaking the readable test layer, which is what keeps large suites maintainable." },
    { q: "What problem do ephemeral, on-demand test environments solve?", options: ["They make tests run without any code", "They avoid the classic problem of one shared QA environment being blocked by another team's half-finished change", "They eliminate the need for test data entirely", "They replace the need for a staging environment"], correct: 1, explain: "Spinning up a fresh environment per pull request or test run (via containers/orchestration) avoids contention and flakiness caused by a single shared, mutable QA environment." },
    { q: "What does service virtualization provide in a test environment?", options: ["A real, fully deployed copy of every dependency", "A simulated stand-in for a real dependency that returns realistic canned or rule-based responses", "A way to skip testing external integrations entirely", "A virtual machine to run the whole test suite on"], correct: 1, explain: "Tools like WireMock and Mountebank simulate a dependency's responses, making tests faster and more reliable when a real dependency is expensive, risky, or not yet built." },
    { q: "Why are tests staged from fast/cheap to slow/expensive in a CI/CD pipeline (unit before integration before E2E)?", options: ["It's arbitrary and doesn't matter", "So a broken build fails fast, before burning time on slower stages", "Because slow tests are always more reliable", "Because CI/CD tools require this specific order"], correct: 1, explain: "Staging cheap, fast checks first means a broken build is caught in seconds or minutes rather than after a much longer, more expensive stage has already run." },
    { q: "What is a 'quality gate' in a CI/CD pipeline?", options: ["A physical security checkpoint for the server room", "An automated check that blocks a merge or deployment when a condition (e.g. pass rate, coverage, security findings) isn't met", "A synonym for a staging environment", "A manual approval step performed only by the QA lead"], correct: 1, explain: "A quality gate is an automated pass/fail condition — like a minimum pass rate or zero critical vulnerabilities — that blocks progress through the pipeline when not satisfied." },
    { q: "What is 'shift-right' testing, as distinct from shift-left?", options: ["Testing only right before a deadline", "Deliberately extending testing into production via canary releases, feature flags, and synthetic monitoring", "Moving all testers to a different time zone", "A technique for right-aligning test reports"], correct: 1, explain: "Shift-right complements shift-left by extending testing into production itself — canary releases, feature flags, and synthetic monitoring validate behavior with real traffic, safely and gradually." },
    { q: "Why does traditional full-stack end-to-end testing stop scaling well for microservices architectures?", options: ["Microservices cannot be tested at all", "The number of service-version combinations grows combinatorially, making full-environment E2E runs slow, flaky, and expensive", "E2E tests are illegal for microservices", "Microservices don't have APIs to test"], correct: 1, explain: "As independently-deployed services multiply, testing every combination end-to-end becomes combinatorially expensive — which is why contract testing exists as an alternative." },
    { q: "How does consumer-driven contract testing (e.g. Pact) avoid needing a full integration environment?", options: ["It doesn't — it still requires deploying every service together", "The consumer defines the interactions it expects, and the provider verifies it satisfies that contract independently, without the consumer needing to run", "It replaces all other forms of testing", "It only works for synchronous REST APIs, never events"], correct: 1, explain: "Each side of the contract is verified independently against the agreed expectations, catching breaking changes without standing up the full chain of services together." },
    { q: "What does chaos engineering do?", options: ["Randomly deletes production data to test backups", "Deliberately injects controlled failures (killed instances, network latency, resource exhaustion) to verify a system degrades gracefully instead of cascading into an outage", "Generates random test data for unit tests", "Introduces bugs into code on purpose to train new developers"], correct: 1, explain: "Chaos engineering (popularized by tools like Chaos Monkey and Gremlin) deliberately injects failure under controlled conditions to verify resilience, rather than waiting for an uncontrolled real outage to find the same gaps." },
    { q: "What does the 'ice-cream cone' anti-pattern describe?", options: ["A perfectly balanced test suite", "A suite top-heavy with slow, brittle UI/E2E tests and too few fast unit tests — the inverse of a healthy Test Pyramid", "A tool for visualizing test coverage", "A pattern for organizing glossary terms"], correct: 1, explain: "The ice-cream cone is the inverse of the Test Pyramid (Module 7): heavy on slow, flaky, high-level UI tests and light on fast, cheap unit tests, making the whole suite slow and hard to maintain." },
    { q: "What is the purpose of a test reporting tool like Allure or ReportPortal?", options: ["To write test cases automatically", "To turn raw test-runner output into readable, historical dashboards showing trends over time", "To replace the need for a CI/CD pipeline", "To generate glossary definitions"], correct: 1, explain: "Tools like Allure and ReportPortal turn raw pass/fail output into historical, trend-visible dashboards — showing whether the pass rate is improving or declining over many builds, not just the latest run." },
    { q: "Why does flaky test detection matter for test reporting infrastructure?", options: ["It has no real value", "Tests that fail intermittently with no code change erode trust in the suite if not flagged and addressed", "It makes tests run faster automatically", "It only matters for manual testing, not automation"], correct: 1, explain: "An intermittently-failing test that isn't flagged as flaky quietly erodes trust in the whole suite, since people stop believing failures mean anything real." }
  ],
  "security-testing": [
    { q: "How does security testing's core question differ from typical functional testing?", options: ["They ask exactly the same question", "Functional testing asks if the happy path works; security testing asks what happens when someone deliberately tries to break the rules", "Security testing only applies to mobile apps", "Functional testing is always more important than security testing"], correct: 1, explain: "Functional testing verifies intended behavior; security testing deliberately asks what happens when a user or attacker tries to make the system do something it was never meant to do." },
    { q: "What does the principle of 'least privilege' mean?", options: ["Every user should have full administrator access by default", "Every user, service, or process should have only the minimum access it needs to do its job", "Privileges should never be reviewed once granted", "Only the security team needs any access controls"], correct: 1, explain: "Least privilege means granting only the minimum necessary access — almost every serious breach involves something having more access than it actually needed." },
    { q: "Which OWASP Top 10 risk is described as: users can act outside their intended permissions, e.g. viewing another user's data by changing an ID in a URL?", options: ["Cryptographic Failures", "Broken Access Control", "Security Misconfiguration", "Server-Side Request Forgery"], correct: 1, explain: "Broken Access Control covers failures to properly restrict what an authenticated user can access or do — the classic example is changing an ID in a URL to view someone else's data." },
    { q: "What is the classic test for SQL Injection in a login field?", options: ["Entering a very long random string", "Entering a payload like ' OR '1'='1 to see if it alters the query's logic and bypasses authentication", "Submitting the form twice quickly", "Leaving the field completely empty"], correct: 1, explain: "A payload like ' OR '1'='1 tests whether untrusted input is unsafely concatenated into a SQL query, potentially altering its logic to bypass the login check entirely." },
    { q: "What distinguishes Stored XSS from Reflected XSS?", options: ["There is no real difference between them", "Stored XSS persists the malicious script (e.g. in a comment) and serves it to other users later; Reflected XSS bounces it back immediately in a response", "Reflected XSS only happens in mobile apps", "Stored XSS cannot be tested for"], correct: 1, explain: "Stored XSS saves the malicious script somewhere it will later be rendered to other users (like a comment field); Reflected XSS immediately reflects the payload back in the same response, often via a crafted URL." },
    { q: "What does CSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery) exploit?", options: ["A weakness in database indexing", "A logged-in user's browser being tricked into submitting an unwanted request to a site where they're authenticated", "A flaw in HTTPS encryption itself", "Slow server response times"], correct: 1, explain: "CSRF tricks a victim's browser — already authenticated to a target site — into submitting a request the victim never intended, such as an invisible auto-submitting form." },
    { q: "What is the key difference between SAST and DAST?", options: ["They are identical approaches with different names", "SAST scans source code without running it; DAST attacks a running application from the outside like a real attacker", "DAST only works on databases", "SAST can only be run once per year"], correct: 1, explain: "SAST analyzes source code statically (no execution needed, can run on every commit); DAST tests a running, deployed application dynamically from the outside, catching different classes of issues." },
    { q: "What does SCA (Software Composition Analysis) scan for?", options: ["Typos in code comments", "Known vulnerabilities (CVEs) in third-party libraries and dependencies", "CSS styling errors", "Database query performance"], correct: 1, explain: "SCA tools like Snyk and OWASP Dependency-Check scan third-party dependencies for known, published vulnerabilities — directly addressing the 'Vulnerable & Outdated Components' OWASP risk." },
    { q: "In penetration testing, what must always be in place before testing begins?", options: ["Nothing — anyone can pen test any system at any time", "A signed agreement defining scope and explicit written authorization", "A fully automated scanning tool", "A public announcement of the test"], correct: 1, explain: "Without a signed agreement and explicit authorization defining what's in scope, the exact same actions used in a pen test are simply illegal, unauthorized hacking." },
    { q: "In the STRIDE threat modeling framework, what does the 'R' stand for?", options: ["Reliability", "Repudiation — denying having performed an action, with no way to prove otherwise", "Redundancy", "Recovery"], correct: 1, explain: "Repudiation in STRIDE refers to a threat where an actor can deny having performed an action and the system has no way to prove otherwise (e.g. missing audit logs)." },
    { q: "What does 'zero trust' mean as an architectural/testing principle?", options: ["No system should ever be tested", "Never assume a request is safe just because it came from inside the network perimeter — every request is authenticated and authorized on its own merits", "Trust every internal service by default", "Only external-facing APIs need authentication"], correct: 1, explain: "Zero trust means every request — even from inside the network — must prove who it is and what it's authorized to do, rather than being trusted automatically because of its source." },
    { q: "Why is verifying that a 'delete my account' request actually deletes data (including backups/replicas) a compliance-relevant testing concern?", options: ["It's purely a performance testing concern", "It relates to data-deletion rights under regulations like GDPR", "It only matters for internal test environments", "It has no relationship to compliance"], correct: 1, explain: "GDPR-adjacent regulations often require that a user's data deletion request actually removes their data across the system, including backups on their retention schedule — testing this end-to-end is a real compliance concern, not just a data-hygiene nitpick." }
  ],
  "performance-ux-automation": [
    { q: "What is the key difference between Load Testing and Stress Testing?", options: ["They are identical techniques with different names", "Load Testing checks behavior under expected traffic; Stress Testing keeps increasing load past expected levels to find where the system breaks", "Stress Testing only applies to mobile apps", "Load Testing is always run in production"], correct: 1, explain: "Load Testing validates behavior at expected, realistic traffic; Stress Testing deliberately pushes past that to find the system's breaking point and how it degrades." },
    { q: "What does Soak Testing specifically catch that a short load test typically misses?", options: ["Typos in the UI", "Memory leaks and slow resource exhaustion that only appear over a long, sustained duration", "Incorrect color contrast", "SQL injection vulnerabilities"], correct: 1, explain: "Soak Testing runs moderate load for many hours or days, surfacing memory leaks and gradual resource exhaustion that a short test simply doesn't run long enough to reveal." },
    { q: "Why is average response time potentially misleading as a performance metric on its own?", options: ["Averages are never useful in any context", "A low average can still hide a meaningful percentage of users experiencing much slower responses — this is why p95/p99 percentiles are reported", "Response time cannot be measured accurately", "Average is only relevant for stress testing, not load testing"], correct: 1, explain: "A system averaging 200ms could still leave 5% of users waiting several seconds — p95/p99 percentiles reveal that tail-end experience that an average alone hides." },
    { q: "What is a 'think-aloud protocol' in usability testing?", options: ["A rule preventing testers from speaking during a session", "Asking a participant to narrate their thoughts as they complete a task, surfacing confusion silent observation alone would miss", "A scripted list of questions read verbatim to the user", "An automated tool that reads UI text aloud for accessibility testing"], correct: 1, explain: "A think-aloud protocol has the participant verbalize their thinking as they work through a task, revealing confusion or hesitation that just watching their actions wouldn't show." },
    { q: "How does A/B testing differ from heuristic evaluation as a UX method?", options: ["They are the same thing", "A/B testing measures actual behavior at scale with real production traffic split between variants, rather than expert judgment or a small observed session", "Heuristic evaluation requires more users than A/B testing", "A/B testing replaces the need for any other UX method"], correct: 1, explain: "A/B testing compares real user behavior at scale between two variants; heuristic evaluation is an expert review against known usability principles — the two are complementary, not substitutes." },
    { q: "Why is a hardcoded fixed sleep (e.g. wait(2000)) considered bad practice in test automation?", options: ["It always makes tests pass regardless of the app's state", "It's either too short (still flaky) or too long (slows the suite) — modern tools auto-wait for an element to become actionable instead", "Fixed sleeps are illegal in most automation frameworks", "It only affects performance tests, not functional ones"], correct: 1, explain: "A fixed sleep is a guess — too short and the test remains flaky, too long and it needlessly slows the whole suite; auto-waiting (built into Playwright/Cypress) waits exactly as long as needed." },
    { q: "Why is a locator tied to a data-testid attribute generally more reliable than one tied to a CSS styling class?", options: ["data-testid attributes run faster than CSS selectors", "A styling class can change during a redesign and silently break the test, while a dedicated test-id attribute is stable across visual changes", "CSS selectors cannot be used in any automation framework", "There is no real difference between the two approaches"], correct: 1, explain: "A CSS class tied to styling is likely to change when a designer restyles the page, breaking the test; a dedicated data-testid attribute is meant only for testing and stays stable across visual redesigns." },
    { q: "Why does test independence (not depending on another test's execution or order) matter in an automated suite?", options: ["It doesn't matter as long as all tests eventually pass", "Order-dependent tests produce a common, hard-to-diagnose 'works alone, fails in the suite' failure pattern", "Independent tests always run slower than dependent ones", "It's only a concern for manual testing, not automation"], correct: 1, explain: "A test that silently depends on another test's leftover state can pass in isolation but fail when run as part of the full suite (or in a different order) — a notoriously hard bug to track down." },
    { q: "What kind of tools does BrowserStack or Sauce Labs provide?", options: ["AI-based test case generation", "Cross-browser/device cloud testing — running the same suite against real browsers/OS/device combinations without maintaining a physical device lab", "Static code analysis for security vulnerabilities", "Bug tracking and defect management"], correct: 1, explain: "BrowserStack, Sauce Labs, and LambdaTest provide cloud access to real browser/OS/device combinations, avoiding the cost and maintenance of a physical device lab." },
    { q: "What is the trade-off typically associated with low-code/record-and-playback automation tools like Katalon Studio?", options: ["They cannot be used by any team, ever", "They lower the barrier to entry for teams without dedicated automation engineers, but are typically less flexible than code-first frameworks for complex logic", "They are always more expensive than code-first frameworks", "They only work for API testing, never UI testing"], correct: 1, explain: "Low-code tools make automation accessible to teams without deep coding skill, but that accessibility usually trades off against the flexibility a code-first framework offers for complex test logic." },
    { q: "What does 'continuous testing' mean, and how does it connect to Module 9's pipeline architecture?", options: ["Running the full manual test suite once a year", "Running automated tests continuously throughout the pipeline, not just before release, so feedback arrives within minutes of a change", "A synonym for exploratory testing", "Testing that never has a defined pass/fail criteria"], correct: 1, explain: "Continuous testing embeds automated tests throughout the CI/CD pipeline so every change gets fast feedback — the tools in this module only deliver value when they're actually wired into a pipeline that runs them continuously." }
  ]
};
